### The session cookie is marked Secure, so it is only ever set over HTTPS.
### Pointing this at the http:// port makes sign-in appear to succeed while the
### browser silently discards the cookie and every later request arrives anonymous.
@ExamArchive_HostAddress = https://localhost:7294

### ==================== Sign in ====================
### Browsing, searching and uploading require an account; reviewing takes a staff one.
### The staff accounts below exist only in Development and are created by SeedData;
### the password is printed as a warning at startup.
### Everything under "Review" below returns 401 until one of these has run — and
### 401 as well for an ordinary account, which cannot tell the two apart on purpose.

### Sign in as a moderator — unlocks the review section
POST {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "moderator",
  "password": "Lozinka!12345"
}

### Sign in as an admin — same powers today, plus the configuration work to come
POST {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "admin",
  "password": "Lozinka!12345"
}

### Wrong password — same message as an unknown username, on purpose
POST {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "moderator",
  "password": "wrong"
}

### Who am I? 401 when not signed in — the cookie is HttpOnly, so this is the
### only way a frontend can find out.
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/me
Accept: application/json

### Change your own password. Requires the current one — a session cookie proves
### the browser signed in once, not that the person at the keyboard owns the
### account. Minimum 12 characters for the new one.
POST {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/change-password
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "currentPassword": "Lozinka!12345",
  "newPassword": "NovaLozinka2026"
}

### Same password back again — refused, so a change that did nothing is not
### mistaken for one that worked
POST {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/change-password
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "currentPassword": "NovaLozinka2026",
  "newPassword": "NovaLozinka2026"
}

### Sign out
POST {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/logout

### ==================== Browsing (signed in) ====================

### Every level of study — the top of the hierarchy, and the first dropdown in a
### search. Takes no filter: there is nothing above it to narrow it by.
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/studies
Accept: application/json

### Majors in Bachelor's
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/majors?studiesId=1
Accept: application/json

### Majors in Master's
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/majors?studiesId=2
Accept: application/json

### Every major
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/majors
Accept: application/json

### Subjects in Computer Science (id 1) — note YearOfStudy
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/subjects?majorId=1
Accept: application/json

### Subjects in Software Engineering (id 2) — Databases lands a year later here
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/subjects?majorId=2
Accept: application/json

### Authorized papers for Databases (id 5) — all approved rows plus this user's
### own pending/rejected rows
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?subjectId=5
Accept: application/json

### Authorized papers for Data Structures (id 4)
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?subjectId=4
Accept: application/json

### Linear Algebra (id 2) — taught in two majors, but the archive is empty
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?subjectId=2
Accept: application/json

### ==================== Searching (signed in) ====================
### The search form is a cascade — Degree, then Major, then Year of study, then
### Subject, each one filling the next. Every step is a request holding the id the
### step before it returned:
###
###   /api/studies                       -> Bachelor's (1)
###   /api/majors?studiesId=1            -> Computer Science (1)
###   /api/subjects?majorId=1            -> the curriculum, every row carrying its
###                                         year, so the Year and Subject dropdowns
###                                         come out of this one response
###   /api/papers?...                    -> the papers
###
### Only subjectId narrows the papers: a paper belongs to a subject and to nothing
### else. studiesId, majorId and yearOfStudy come along because the subject cannot
### remember them — Databases is taught in three majors, in two different years —
### so a reloaded page has nothing to rebuild its dropdowns from without them.
### They are verified rather than used, which is what makes them worth trusting.

### The whole cascade: Bachelor's > Computer Science > 2nd year > Databases
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?studiesId=1&majorId=1&yearOfStudy=2&subjectId=5
Accept: application/json

### The same subject reached through Software Engineering, where it lands a year
### later — a different path, the same papers
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?studiesId=1&majorId=2&yearOfStudy=3&subjectId=5
Accept: application/json

### Optional narrowing, once the subject is chosen: exam type, year, month. These
### are the only parameters after subjectId that change what comes back.
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?studiesId=1&majorId=1&yearOfStudy=2&subjectId=5&examType=Final
Accept: application/json

### Nothing matches — an empty page with honest totals, not a 404. The search was
### valid, the archive simply has no such paper, and the client can loosen one
### filter rather than start the cascade again.
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?subjectId=5&examType=Resit&year=2023
Accept: application/json

### ---- Paths that contradict themselves — 400, not a silently wrong answer ----

### Right subject, wrong year: Databases is 2nd year in Computer Science, not 3rd.
### The error names the real year, so a stale bookmark can be repaired rather than
### only reported broken.
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?studiesId=1&majorId=1&yearOfStudy=3&subjectId=5
Accept: application/json

### Electrical Engineering (id 3) does not teach Databases at all
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?studiesId=1&majorId=3&subjectId=5
Accept: application/json

### Computer Science is a Bachelor's major, so it is not in Master's (id 2)
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?studiesId=2&majorId=1&subjectId=5
Accept: application/json

### A path with nothing to describe — refused rather than quietly handing back the
### whole archive, which would look like it had worked
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?majorId=1
Accept: application/json

### Year of study without a major: the same subject sits in different years in
### different majors, so there is no row this could be checked against
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?yearOfStudy=2&subjectId=5
Accept: application/json

### ---- Subjects, filtered server-side ----
### The search form will not usually need this — it asks for the whole curriculum
### once and filters in the browser, so the Year and Subject dropdowns respond
### without a round trip. Here for a caller that would rather ask.
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/subjects?majorId=1&yearOfStudy=2
Accept: application/json

### ==================== Pagination ====================
### Every listing answers with {"data": [...], "meta": {page, perPage, totalItems,
### totalPages}} — the rows alone never say whether there are more. Ten per page
### unless asked otherwise, and 100 is the ceiling.
###
### The two knobs are `page` and `perPage`, named for the keys they come back as,
### so a client can send back what it was handed.

### Second page of this caller's authorized paper set, five at a time
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?page=2&perPage=5
Accept: application/json

### Over the ceiling — served 100, and meta.perPage says so rather than a 400
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?perPage=1000
Accept: application/json

### Past the end — an empty data array with honest totals, not a 404, so a client
### holding a stale page number can see it has overshot and step back
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?page=999
Accept: application/json

### Nonsense values are clamped, not rejected: page 0 is page 1
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?page=0&perPage=0
Accept: application/json

### Reference lists are paged too — ask for a full page to fill a dropdown in one
### request rather than looping
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/majors?perPage=100
Accept: application/json

### One approved paper with its pages. The pages arrive grouped by format under "files" —
### {"pdf": [...], "jpg": [...]} — with one array per format the paper actually
### has, so a missing key and an empty array mean the same thing.
###
### NOTE: nothing serves the file bytes any more. /api/papers/{id}/files and
### /api/papers/{id}/files/{pageNumber} were both removed, and this response
### describes the pages without handing any of them over.
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers/1
Accept: application/json

### Check a submission without a login using the claim code from the upload response.
### Replace the code below with the "claimToken" the upload actually returned —
### these are random, so this exact one will 404.
### Case, dashes and O-for-0 are all forgiven, so it can be typed as written down.
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers/status/DHA22-9Z2JC-0KDFM-73RZ3
Accept: application/json

### An unknown code — same 404 as a malformed one, on purpose
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers/status/AAAAA-BBBBB-CCCCC-DDDDD
Accept: application/json

### ==================== Account management (Admin only) ====================
### Sign in as admin first. A moderator gets 403 on everything here.

### Every staff account, including deactivated ones
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/users
Accept: application/json

### Create an account for a professor. The response carries a generated password —
### it is shown once and never again, so read it out before closing the window.
### The account can sign in but do nothing else until it replaces that password.
POST {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "prof.jovanovic",
  "role": 3
}

### Promote to admin. This is how the archive stops depending on one administrator.
POST {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/users/3/role
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "role": 2
}

### Somebody forgot their password. This is the whole of password recovery.
POST {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/users/3/reset-password

### Somebody left. Deactivate rather than delete, so their past decisions keep
### their name on them. Refused with 409 if this is the last active admin.
POST {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/users/3/deactivate

### Somebody came back. The password is untouched.
POST {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/users/3/activate

### ==================== Review (Moderator or Admin) ====================
### These are the same /api/papers routes as above — there is no separate
### moderation prefix any more. What changes is the role on the session cookie:
### approving/rejecting below returns 401 without a staff sign-in. Listing with a
### status remains available to an ordinary user, but only for that user's own rows.

### The review queue, longest-waiting first. Staff receive the global queue; an
### ordinary user making this request receives only their own pending submissions.
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?status=Pending
Accept: application/json

### Papers already rejected, with the reason each was turned down for
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers?status=Rejected
Accept: application/json

### One pending paper. The same route the public uses — it 404s for them and
### answers for staff.
GET {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers/28
Accept: application/json

### A reviewer can see what a submission is made of — page count, formats, sizes —
### but not open any of it, now that the byte-serving route is gone.

### Approve a paper — it becomes visible to everyone, signed in or not
POST {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers/28/approve

### Reject a paper. The reason is required and shown to the submitter.
POST {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers/29/reject
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "reason": "Page 2 is upside down and unreadable. Please re-photograph it."
}

### Correct a paper's filing details rather than rejecting a perfectly good scan
PUT {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers/28
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "subjectId": 5,
  "examType": "Final",
  "month": 6,
  "year": 2024
}

### Delete a paper and its files. Administrator only — a moderator gets 401 here
### even though every other route in this section answers for them.
DELETE {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers/28

### Staff upload — published immediately, skips the queue.
### The same /api/papers/upload an ordinary account posts to; the role on the
### cookie is what decides whether the paper lands Approved or Pending. The most
### dangerous endpoint here: for staff it writes to the public archive with no review.
###
### Limits, whoever is uploading: at most 10 pages, 20 MB per file, 100 MB in total,
### and formats .pdf .jpg .jpeg .png .webp confirmed against the bytes, not the name.
### Formats may be mixed freely — a scanned PDF alongside photographed pages is one
### paper — with the single extra rule that at most 2 of the 10 may be PDFs.
POST {{ExamArchive_HostAddress}}/api/papers/upload
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=----Boundary

------Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="SubjectId"

5
------Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="ExamType"

Final
------Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="Month"

6
------Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="Year"

2026
------Boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="Files"; filename="page1.pdf"
Content-Type: application/pdf

< ./uploads/2026/databases-final-2026-01-12b6010d.pdf
------Boundary--

###
